{
  "dom_id": "step-q6677",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}-19{x}^{2}-3x+2?$",
  "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $2,$ and the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of\u00a0}2}{\\text{factor of\u00a0}8}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{1}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(1)$ is a factor of $2$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $8.$",
